Why Is Glass Core Substrate Technology Gaining Attention?

Glass core substrates provide a platform for packaging semiconductor devices while offering properties such as dimensional stability, electrical insulation and the potential for fine-pitch interconnections.

Through-glass vias create vertical electrical pathways through the substrate. These connections can support high-density packaging architectures in which multiple components must communicate through compact interconnect structures.

As semiconductor packages become more complex, material selection is becoming an important consideration for electrical performance, manufacturing yield and package reliability.

AI and High-Performance Computing Support Market Development

AI accelerators and high-performance computing devices require advanced packaging solutions to connect processors, memory and other components. As bandwidth requirements increase, packaging designers are evaluating materials and architectures that can support dense interconnections and efficient signal transmission.

Glass core substrates are being developed for applications where electrical characteristics and dimensional stability are important. Their commercial adoption depends on manufacturing maturity, via formation, substrate handling and qualification by packaging customers.

Through-Glass Via Manufacturing and Panel-Level Packaging

TGV production can involve laser drilling, laser modification followed by etching, wet etching and other specialized processing techniques. The objective is to form precise vias while maintaining substrate integrity.

Panel-level packaging is another area of interest because larger processing formats may improve production economics when manufacturing processes can be scaled reliably. However, large glass panels also introduce challenges involving handling, warpage, via quality and defect control.

